Branch 1: What, When and Where Reflection

During the Security and Risk Management (SRM_PCOM7E) module, I engaged in a structured learning experience that unfolded across twelve weekly units. Each unit contributed to building a layered understanding of how organisations anticipate, model, and manage risks in increasingly digitalised environments. The timeline of my work began with foundational concepts in Unit 1 and culminated with practical applications in Units 11 and 12, where I submitted both an Executive Summary Project and the e-Portfolio, respectively.

From the outset in Unit 1, I encountered core principles of risk, threats, and vulnerabilities. These formed the conceptual vocabulary that informed all subsequent tasks. For example, learning the distinction between internal and external threat actors helped me interpret real-life cyber incidents critically, as discussed in Unit 2. Units 3 to 5 expanded my technical knowledge about threat modelling and qualitative risk assessments, which later became essential for the Development Team Risk Report. The BIA activity in Unit 6 was particularly valuable—it helped me grasp the prioritisation of business functions under crisis scenarios.

The “when” aspect of my learning was significantly reinforced through the module deadlines. Having to deliver the Development Team Report by 8 September 2025 and the Individual Executive Summary by 13 October 2025 encouraged me to apply time management and structured planning. These key dates served not only as submission deadlines but also as milestones for consolidating knowledge and integrating peer and tutor feedback.

The “where” component of this reflection relates to the virtual learning environment and digital tools used throughout the course. I regularly engaged with the UoEO Learning Platform, where I accessed academic readings, submitted assignments, and participated in peer evaluations. Importantly, I applied these learnings in virtual collaboration spaces like Microsoft Teams and shared cloud-based documents when preparing group work, mimicking remote cybersecurity team settings in industry.

A pivotal moment was when our group, assigned to analyse cyber threats in the Pampered Pets case study, used collaborative tools to distribute tasks and cross-check our risk assessments. We combined real-world frameworks—such as the ISO/IEC 27005 standard—with case-specific insights, which further grounded our learning in practice. This connection between course content and real-world application was especially powerful in demonstrating the relevance of our studies to future employment in the cybersecurity sector.

Reflecting on the “what,” “when,” and “where” of my learning, I can confidently say that this module not only delivered theoretical knowledge but also equipped me with applied skills through structured timelines, collaborative tools, and practice-led activities. It showed me that cybersecurity risk management is not simply about IT controls; it is an organisational capability that must be embedded into people, processes, and strategic priorities.
